WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
INTRO:
Do you know what the most profitable company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.780
in the world is? Most people would probably guess&nbsp;
Apple. This makes sense given that Apple is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.700
of the most recognizable brands in the entire&nbsp;
world and they’re usually the largest company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.960
in the world as well. But, Apple doesn’t hold&nbsp;
the title of most profitable company. This title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.960
actually goes to a background company called Saudi&nbsp;
Aramco. If you’re not familiar with Saudi Aramco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.480
they’re Saudi Arabia’s flagship oil company and&nbsp;
they’ve been at the top of the fortune 500 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:40.080
decades. Historically, they’ve often been the most&nbsp;
profitable company in the world, but their profits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.620
over the past 12 months are unlike anything we’ve&nbsp;
ever seen before. By now, I’m sure you’re well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.140
aware of the sky high gas prices around the world.&nbsp;
While things have cooled down from their peak a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.720
few months ago, prices are still pretty high on&nbsp;
a historical basis. This has been terrible news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:03.360
for everyday people who are just trying to make&nbsp;
ends meet, but for oil companies, this has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:10.380
a godsend. At the peak, Saudi Arabia was profiting&nbsp;
over a $1 billion per day just from oil exports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:18.540
And Saudi Aramco’s most recent 12 month earnings&nbsp;
stand at an eye watering $279 billion. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:25.380
perspective, this is well over two times Apple’s&nbsp;
earnings of $120 billion. It’s also nearly as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:31.980
as the profits of Apple, Google, and Microsoft&nbsp;
combined. Something else to note is that it hasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:39.000
even been one year since oil prices really became&nbsp;
heated. So, as we close of 2022, Saudi Aramco’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:45.420
rolling 12 month profits should just balloon even&nbsp;
higher, potentially to over $300 billion. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:51.000
that amount of money, Saudi Aramco could literally&nbsp;
buy Samsung with a single check and still have $30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.720
billion leftover. But things haven’t always been&nbsp;
so rosy for Saudi Arabia. In fact, just a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.940
years ago, much of Saudi Arabia was just a bunch&nbsp;
of tribes who were fighting for survival. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:08.220
you might say that they just got lucky with oil,&nbsp;
and while they did get lucky with their natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.160
resources, that in itself is nowhere near enough&nbsp;
to build such an empire. And if you don’t believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.860
me just take a look at Venezuela who not only&nbsp;
has more oil than Saudi Arabia but discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.840
their oil decades earlier. You would think&nbsp;
that such a country would be filthy rich but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.640
they’re unfortuantley dirt poor and riddled with&nbsp;
hyper inflation. The same thing could be said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.160
about Canada as well who has the world’s third&nbsp;
largest oil reserves. Canada is of course by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.160
no means doing bad, but given that they’ve been an&nbsp;
advanced country for far longer than Saudi Arabia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.780
you would think that they would’ve been able&nbsp;
to capitalize on their oil way more than Saudi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:51.780
Arabia. Yet, that’s not the case, so how did&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia become the winners of the oil game?

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.980
DESPERATE SEARCH:
Taking a look back, Saudi Arabia was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:05.220
by no means early to the oil game. In fact, they&nbsp;
were rather late and their entry into oil wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:11.400
particularly strategic either. It was more of just&nbsp;
a desperate gamble. In the decades preceding Saudi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:17.280
Arabia’s search for oil, Saudi Arabia wasn’t even&nbsp;
a thing. Instead, the area was controlled by two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.740
families called the house of Saud and the house of&nbsp;
Rashid who were constantly in conflict. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.140
the house of Saud would be on top and other times&nbsp;
the house of Rashid would be on top, but there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.960
really no winner. It was more or less just a big&nbsp;
power struggle that led nowhere. This, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.540
would all change with the turn of the century into&nbsp;
the 1900s. At the time, the Rashidi family was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:45.840
on top and the leader of the Saudi family, Abdul&nbsp;
Aziz Al Saud was in exile in Kuwait. Abdul hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.100
forgotten about the house of Rashid though, and he&nbsp;
was simply plotting his counterattack which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:55.920
take place in 1902. With his initial strike, Abdul&nbsp;
would take back Riyadh which was just the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.860
of a series of conquests and before you knew it,&nbsp;
the Sauds were back in control. The Rashids were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:08.100
forced into exile just like the Sauds previously,&nbsp;
but this was by no means the end of it. The Saudi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.500
family knew that the Rashidi family could strike&nbsp;
back at any time just like Sauds did. So, they set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.720
out on advancing the area so much that the Rashidi&nbsp;
family couldn’t form a counterattack. Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.360
this turned out be a non issue given that the&nbsp;
Rashidi family would dissolve in 1921. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.160
the Sauds didn’t know that at the time, so&nbsp;
they started working on creating an unbeatable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:34.500
force. And the first step in this journey was to&nbsp;
actually create an official country. This is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.480
would eventually lead to the birth of Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia in September of 1932. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:45.060
they officially had a country, but this didn’t&nbsp;
mean much without an economy. And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.920
being surrounded by a bunch of sand, the Sauds&nbsp;
didn’t exactly have a bunch of economical options.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.520
In fact, they really only had two options. Option&nbsp;
number 1 was to leverage their access to the sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.860
They could build a port or go pearl diving&nbsp;
or fish, but this is what they had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.660
been doing for millennia. Pearl diving in&nbsp;
the Arabian peninsula literally dates back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:13.380
7000 years, and given their current situation, it&nbsp;
clearly wasn’t all that profitable. Option number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:19.080
2 was to leverage their religious significance.&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia was and still is the home to Mecca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.880
which is where millions of Muslims pilgrimage&nbsp;
to every year. But I suspect that the Sauds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:28.560
felt that leaning too much into religion&nbsp;
would threaten their own power. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.840
they’ve been destroying the city eversince they&nbsp;
took over. In the last 37 years itself, Saudi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.360
Arabia has demolished 95% of Mecca’s historical&nbsp;
buildings. So, clearly, becoming an Islamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.820
Vatican city wasn’t a top consideration. With the&nbsp;
sea and religion crossed off as economic options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.880
Saudi Arabia had to think outside the box. And&nbsp;
it didn’t take them long to take the oil gamble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.920
Just one year after the country was formed, Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia would sign an oil concession agreement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.440
the Standard Oil Company of California or SOCAL.&nbsp;
For many years, SOCAL had little to show for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.840
efforts in Saudi Arabia, but eventually&nbsp;
in 1938, SOCAL would strike liquid gold.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:14.280
EMPIRE BUILDERS:
For many decades, the vast majority of profits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.740
from Saudi Arabia went to American companies while&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia was left with a small dividend. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.600
this might seem unfair at first glance, there&nbsp;
was actually a lot of value for the Sauds in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:29.040
this partnership. Sure, America might be the&nbsp;
ones that were left with the short term profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.840
but it was Saudi Arabia that was left with the&nbsp;
long term investments. Scaling to millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:40.380
barrels per day is by no means cheap and guess who&nbsp;
fronted the cost: the Americans. Access to capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:45.960
was not the only thing that the Americans provided&nbsp;
either. They also provided expertise that simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:51.540
wasn’t available in Saudi Arabia at the time. For&nbsp;
example, in the 1940s, the Americans would help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:58.080
Saudi Arabia build a 1200 km oil pipeline which&nbsp;
was at the time the longest in the world. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.960
Americans also helped Saudi Arabia identify the&nbsp;
Safaniyah oil field in 1951 which is the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:09.480
largest offshore oil field to this day. So,&nbsp;
while America was hogging most of the profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.940
they were simultaneously building an empire,&nbsp;
an empire that Saudi Arabia could eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:21.300
takeover. Saudi Arabia wasn’t in any rush though.&nbsp;
While their entrance into the oil industry was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.980
kind of an accident, they were determined to be&nbsp;
strategic with how they played their cards moving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:32.040
A great example of this was the formation of OPEC&nbsp;
in the 1960. If you’re not familiar with OPEC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:38.040
it’s basically an oil union for oil producing&nbsp;
countries. Anyway, it wasn’t till the 1970s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:43.740
that Saudi Arabia finally began to nationalize&nbsp;
Aramco. Saudi Aramco’s website likes to claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:49.080
that Saudi Arabia acquired Aramco as opposed to&nbsp;
nationalizing the company. The story goes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:56.100
the Saudi government bought a 25% stake in 1973,&nbsp;
followed by a 35% stake in 1974, and finally a 40%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:02.820
stake in 1980. I guess this is technically true,&nbsp;
but it’s unlikely that Saudi Arabia paid anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:08.400
near market price for Aramco. The acquisition&nbsp;
price was never revealed, but experts estimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:14.280
that it was likely somewhere between $1.5 to&nbsp;
$2 billion. Even with inflation, that’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:20.880
about 15 billion 2022 dollars. For perspective,&nbsp;
the company is now worth over $2 trillion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:26.040
oil production hasn’t really increased since the&nbsp;
Americans left. So, did the Americans get ripped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:32.160
off with the acquisition? Probably, but you can’t&nbsp;
feel too bad for them given that they had 40 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.460
to exploit Saudi oil undisturbed. And with that,&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia had officially taken over Aramco.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.640
WHY SAUDI ARABIA WON:&nbsp;

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:48.900
Up to this point, Saudi Arabia’s journey isn’t&nbsp;
all that different from Venezuela’s. Saudi Arabia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:54.540
essentially called in Exxon and let them exploit&nbsp;
them for many decades before finally taking over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.540 --> 00:09:01.080
This is basically identical to what Venezuela did&nbsp;
just with Shell instead of Exxon. If anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:06.840
Venezuela was ahead of the Saudi Arabia when it&nbsp;
came to finding oil and nationalizing it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:12.480
why did these two countries end up on polar ends&nbsp;
of the spectrum. Well, at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:18.960
it all just comes down to management and strategy.&nbsp;
I know most of us aren’t fans of authoritarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.720
regimes like Saudi Arabia, but this is one of the&nbsp;
few cases in which being a dictatorship actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.060
helped the country. You see, Saudi kings and&nbsp;
princes were always able to focus on the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:35.340
picture because they didn’t have to worry about&nbsp;
selfish reasons. As long as the country was doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:41.040
well, their family would more than likely keep&nbsp;
control and become extremely rich themselves. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.260
same, however, could not be said about Venezuelan&nbsp;
leadership. Just because the country does well 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:52.200
to 30 years from now doesn’t mean that their&nbsp;
families would do well. Their time in power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.400
was more than likely limited, so they had to&nbsp;
make the most of it while the still could.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:02.340
In other words, they had to steal as much as they&nbsp;
could while they were still in power. This is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:10.080
a $100 billion was embezzeled by country officials&nbsp;
between 1972 and 1997 alone. And this is also why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.700
Venezuelan gold and currency reserves stand at&nbsp;
just $10 billion while Saudi Arabian reserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:20.640
stand at $473 billion. Aside from making long&nbsp;
term decisions and saving up their money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.340
Saudi Arabia also reinvested this money far more&nbsp;
effectively than Venezuela. Saudi Arabia used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:31.800
their money to build businesses, invest into the&nbsp;
economy, and recruit international talent. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:39.600
why 85% of Saudi engineers and 18% of Saudi CEOs&nbsp;
are actually expats. Vision 2030 is also a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:44.700
example of this. In the meantime, Venezuela was&nbsp;
embracing socialism and spending all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:50.160
money on social programs and subsidizing various&nbsp;
everyday goods. So, as Saudi Arabia was becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:56.580
less dependent on oil, Venezuela was becoming more&nbsp;
dependent on oil. While this works out fine when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:03.840
oil is strong, the same cannot be said when oil&nbsp;
is weak. And that’s exactly what happened in 2014&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.940
when oil prices crashed and the Venezuelan&nbsp;
economy collapsed. While this crash was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:14.100
fun for Saudi Arabia either, they were able to&nbsp;
comfortably make it to the otherside not because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:19.080
of any one decision that they made during the&nbsp;
crash, but because of decades of right decisions.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:23.640
SAUDI ARAMCO TODAY:&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.680
Today, Saudi Aramco is one of the largest&nbsp;
companies in the world and it’s usually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:33.360
most profitable. Now of course, these&nbsp;
insane oil prices wont last forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:39.660
and things will eventually cool down, hopefully.&nbsp;
This means that Saudi Aramco’s profits will also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:45.720
cool down with time, but all this means for them&nbsp;
is profiting a $100 billion per year instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:51.300
$200 or $300 billion. Zooming out to the long&nbsp;
term, the world is slowly moving away from oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:57.360
but this will probably not impact Saudi Arabia all&nbsp;
that much given that they’ve been slowly moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.220
away from oil themselves. And given that they’ve&nbsp;
been playing their cards extremely strategically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:07.800
eversince they found oil, I see no reason why this&nbsp;
will change in the coming decades. Until then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.300
Saudi Arabia will remain as one of the few&nbsp;
countries in which an authoritarian regime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:17.340
actually worked out, atleast economically.&nbsp;
If we’re talking socially, that’s a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:22.680
different story, but that’s a topic for another&nbsp;
video. Did you realize how high Saudi Aramco’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:28.140
profits were? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you hope oil prices come down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:32.700
sooner rather than later. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

